    "candidate_text": "Let \\(p=4t+3\\) be prime and set \\(m=(p-1)/2=2t+1\\), which is odd. All congruences are modulo \\(p\\).\n\nSuppose first that \\(a\\) is a primitive root modulo \\(p\\). Then \\(\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=p-1=2m\\). Since \\((a^m)^2=a^{2m}\\equiv1\\) but \\(a^m\\not\\equiv1\\), and the only roots of \\(x^2\\equiv1\\pmod p\\) are \\(\\pm1\\), we have \\(a^m\\equiv-1\\). Therefore\n\\[\n(-a)^m=(-1)^m a^m\\equiv(-1)(-1)=1.\n\\]\nIf \\((-a)^n\\equiv1\\), then \\(a^{2n}=(-a)^{2n}\\equiv1\\). Hence \\(2m\\mid2n\\), so \\(m\\mid n\\). Thus \\(\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m=(p-1)/2\\).\n\nConversely, suppose \\(\\operatorname{ord}_p(-a)=m\\). Since \\(m\\) is odd,\n\\[\na^m=(-1)^m(-a)^m\\equiv-1,\n\\]\nand hence \\(a^{2m}\\equiv1\\). If \\(a^n\\equiv1\\), then \\((-a)^{2n}=a^{2n}\\equiv1\\), so \\(m\\mid2n\\). Because \\(m\\) is odd, \\(m\\mid n\\); write \\(n=qm\\). Then\n\\[\n1\\equiv a^n=(a^m)^q\\equiv(-1)^q.\n\\]\nSince \\(p\\) is odd, this forces \\(q\\) to be even. Therefore \\(2m\\mid n\\). Consequently \\(\\operatorname{ord}_p(a)=2m=p-1\\), so \\(a\\) is a primitive root modulo \\(p\\).",
